WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.666
Welcome to Gander Oceanic.

00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:09.051
Your application has been reviewed and accepted by senior staff,

00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:13.125
so you are now a student, and this course is the next stage of your training.

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:15.778
It's for controllers who already hold a C1 rating,

00:00:15.778 --> 00:00:18.325
so you don't need to learn control from scratch.

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:22.369
Your job now is to learn how oceanic control works, and ours is to teach you.

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:25.365
Here's where you are on the path.

00:00:25.365 --> 00:00:29.477
Your application and the senior staff review are done, and you've been accepted.

00:00:29.477 --> 00:00:33.126
The Training Hub on the Gander website shows your progress at any time.

00:00:33.126 --> 00:00:35.182
Next, you prepare for the entrance exam.

00:00:35.182 --> 00:00:38.368
Everything in it is in the Knowledge Base and in these videos.

00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:41.695
Then you take the exam when you're ready.

00:00:41.695 --> 00:00:45.402
It's twenty multiple-choice questions, and the pass mark is eighty percent.

00:00:45.402 --> 00:00:47.801
If you don't pass, you can retake after one day,

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.950
and after seven days for any later attempt.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.004
Once you've passed, you submit your availability so an instructor can pick you up.

00:00:55.304 --> 00:00:59.252
Finally, you have a live session with that instructor on the network.

00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:03.229
Under a shared agreement with VATSIM UK, Oceanic students are trained

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.976
for both the Gander and the Shanwick sides of the North Atlantic.

00:01:06.976 --> 00:01:11.096
So your live session is taken on NAT_FSS, the position that covers both.

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:14.100
That way, every competence you need is explained and

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.989
understood before an Oceanic endorsement is given.

00:01:16.989 --> 00:01:21.623
If the instructor passes you, you receive your endorsement and your Discord role.

00:01:22.923 --> 00:01:24.111
There are three videos.

00:01:24.111 --> 00:01:28.140
This one covers both airspaces, Gander and Shanwick, and who you'll work with.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.638
The second covers procedures.

00:01:29.638 --> 00:01:31.808
The third covers communications and tools.

00:01:31.808 --> 00:01:36.043
Before we look at each area, let's step back and look at the whole North Atlantic.

00:01:37.343 --> 00:01:40.474
The North Atlantic, or the NAT, links Europe and North America,

00:01:40.474 --> 00:01:43.655
and in the real world it's one of the busiest oceanic airspaces.

00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:46.088
Remember, though, that this course is for VATSIM,

00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:50.657
so some of the equipment you'll hear about exists in the real world but isn't in place here.

00:01:50.657 --> 00:01:54.602
On VATSIM, the NAT is split between six areas, each run by a different division.

00:01:55.902 --> 00:01:56.934
Here they all are.

00:01:56.934 --> 00:01:59.653
Gander Oceanic, in the west, is part of VATCAN,

00:01:59.653 --> 00:02:03.702
and the overall responsibility for that area sits with Gander Oceanic.

00:02:03.702 --> 00:02:08.462
Shanwick Oceanic, in the east, is managed by VATSIM UK, and the same applies there.

00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:10.813
These are the two you'll be endorsed for.

00:02:10.813 --> 00:02:14.999
To the north, Reykjavik, Bodø and Nuuk are managed by VATSIM Scandinavia.

00:02:14.999 --> 00:02:17.316
Santa Maria is run by the Portugal vACC,

00:02:17.316 --> 00:02:20.906
and New York Oceanic is part of VATUSA, run by New York ARTCC.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:25.354
A flight across the Atlantic often passes through two or more of these areas,

00:02:25.354 --> 00:02:28.993
which is why coordinating with your neighbours matters so much.

00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:31.459
The website for each division is on screen.

00:02:32.759 --> 00:02:35.055
Now a little history from the real world,

00:02:35.055 --> 00:02:38.526
because it explains why oceanic control works the way it does.

00:02:38.526 --> 00:02:40.823
Out over the real ocean there's no radar,

00:02:40.823 --> 00:02:44.183
so for decades controllers couldn't see the aircraft at all.

00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:48.040
Pilots reported their position by H F radio at each oceanic waypoint,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.171
and the controller built the picture from those reports.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:55.799
To keep aircraft apart without seeing them, controllers used procedural separation,

00:02:55.799 --> 00:03:01.097
which means big gaps in time and distance, roughly forty to ninety nautical miles horizontally.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:05.452
In nineteen ninety-seven, the NAT also introduced reduced vertical separation,

00:03:05.452 --> 00:03:09.361
so aircraft could fly one thousand feet apart instead of two thousand.

00:03:10.661 --> 00:03:12.400
Then technology changed things.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.113
In the real world, aircraft began sending their position automatically by satellite.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:17.842
That's ADS-C.

00:03:17.842 --> 00:03:21.129
And pilots and controllers began exchanging text messages,

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:23.397
called CPDLC, instead of talking on H F.

00:03:23.397 --> 00:03:27.186
Today in the real world, flights between flight level two-nine-zero

00:03:27.186 --> 00:03:30.522
and four-one-zero across most of the NAT must use datalink.

00:03:31.822 --> 00:03:34.650
The next big step came on the twenty-seventh of March,

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.626
twenty nineteen, when Gander and Shanwick both began using space-based ADS-B in the real world.

00:03:39.626 --> 00:03:45.479
Satellites pick up the signal from each aircraft, which gives controllers real-time surveillance over the ocean,

00:03:45.479 --> 00:03:51.385
and lets them use separation of as little as fourteen to seventeen nautical miles for suitably equipped aircraft.

00:03:52.685 --> 00:03:54.574
So what does that mean on VATSIM?

00:03:54.574 --> 00:03:59.726
Datalink and CPDLC are not mandatory to cross the NAT here, as they are in the real world.

00:03:59.726 --> 00:04:03.368
Aircraft that aren't ADS-B equipped are welcome to fly the NAT,

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:05.737
and they give you voice position reports.

00:04:05.737 --> 00:04:10.856
The simulation treats ADS-B equipped aircraft as if they had the real-world surveillance,

00:04:10.856 --> 00:04:17.988
datalink and electronic position reports, so their voice position reports are optional and the reduced separation can apply.

00:04:17.988 --> 00:04:21.277
The vNAAATS plugin shows you which type each aircraft is,

00:04:21.277 --> 00:04:25.545
using the equipment code in its flight plan, and a pilot may tell you too.

00:04:25.545 --> 00:04:28.179
Video two covers exactly how separation works.

00:04:28.179 --> 00:04:33.159
Position reports, estimates and procedures are still the foundation of oceanic control.

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:34.934
Now let's go through the areas.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:38.212
Now the areas, starting with Gander.

00:04:38.212 --> 00:04:41.839
The Gander Oceanic FIR borders Shanwick along thirty degrees west.

00:04:41.839 --> 00:04:46.373
It borders Santa Maria at forty-five north, between thirty and forty degrees west,

00:04:46.373 --> 00:04:50.962
and New York Oceanic at forty-five north, between forty and fifty-one degrees west.

00:04:50.962 --> 00:04:55.029
On the land side, it borders Gander Domestic, Montreal, Edmonton and Nuuk.

00:04:56.329 --> 00:05:01.475
All the airspace in the FIR from flight level fifty-five up to unlimited is Class A.

00:05:01.475 --> 00:05:04.415
That's the Gander OCA, the Oceanic Control Area.

00:05:04.415 --> 00:05:07.785
Below flight level fifty-five, the airspace is Class G.

00:05:09.085 --> 00:05:13.024
One more area matters: the Gander Oceanic Transition Area, or GOTA.

00:05:13.024 --> 00:05:16.935
This is airspace that Gander Oceanic delegates to Gander Domestic,

00:05:16.935 --> 00:05:20.490
so the domestic radar controller handles it rather than you.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.548
Also remember the transition level.

00:05:22.548 --> 00:05:26.898
In the North Atlantic oceanic control areas it is flight level fifty-five.

00:05:28.198 --> 00:05:29.379
Now the Shanwick side.

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:32.357
The Shanwick FIR borders Gander at thirty degrees west,

00:05:32.357 --> 00:05:35.930
Santa Maria at forty-five north, and Reykjavik at sixty-one north.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:39.456
On the eastern side it borders the domestic airspace of Scottish,

00:05:39.456 --> 00:05:41.300
Shannon, London, Brest and Madrid.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.313
As in Gander, the airspace from flight level fifty-five upward is Class A,

00:05:45.313 --> 00:05:46.831
and that's the Shanwick OCA.

00:05:46.831 --> 00:05:48.657
Below it, the airspace is Class G.

00:05:49.957 --> 00:05:52.624
Shanwick also delegates three transition areas,

00:05:52.624 --> 00:05:55.803
all above flight level fifty-five, to radar controllers.

00:05:55.803 --> 00:05:57.903
The Northern Oceanic Transition Area,

00:05:57.903 --> 00:06:01.706
NOTA, and the Shannon Oceanic Transition Area, SOTA, go to Shannon.

00:06:01.706 --> 00:06:04.798
The Brest Oceanic Transition Area, BOTA, goes to Brest.

00:06:04.798 --> 00:06:06.956
Just like the GOTA on the Gander side,

00:06:06.956 --> 00:06:10.138
these areas belong to the radar controllers, not to you.

00:06:11.438 --> 00:06:15.831
Gander Oceanic Control manages the en-route traffic in the Gander OCA.

00:06:15.831 --> 00:06:20.915
The main position is CZQO_FSS, and on the radio, Gander is known as Gander Radio.

00:06:20.915 --> 00:06:23.070
Shanwick follows the same pattern,

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:27.316
with EGGX_CTR as its main position and the callsign Shanwick Radio.

00:06:27.316 --> 00:06:31.814
Each position has its own VHF alias frequency and its own HF frequency,

00:06:31.814 --> 00:06:34.094
and you'll find them all in the SOP.

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:38.797
On major events, extra positions open on both sides: Alpha,

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:44.161
Bravo, Charlie, Delta and Foxtrot, plus the Delivery positions that issue oceanic clearances.

00:06:44.161 --> 00:06:48.402
We'll only skim over these, because you'll rarely see them outside events.

00:06:48.402 --> 00:06:49.720
The SOP lists them all.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.101
Every Gander position uses the same callsign: Gander Radio.

00:06:54.401 --> 00:06:55.771
Two things to remember.

00:06:55.771 --> 00:06:59.799
CZQO_FSS must be staffed before any other Gander position can open,

00:06:59.799 --> 00:07:02.624
and on the Shanwick side, EGGX_CTR comes first.

00:07:02.624 --> 00:07:05.306
Then there's NAT_FSS, called the NAT Bandbox.

00:07:05.306 --> 00:07:09.572
When local controllers are offline, it covers both Shanwick and Gander,

00:07:09.572 --> 00:07:12.695
using whichever callsign fits where the aircraft is.

00:07:12.695 --> 00:07:16.033
It's also the position you'll use for your live session.

00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:19.211
Because NAT routes change every day,

00:07:19.211 --> 00:07:22.393
there are no published sector boundaries between controllers.

00:07:22.393 --> 00:07:27.023
If you split the airspace north and south, you agree the split with the other controller,

00:07:27.023 --> 00:07:29.364
then coordinate it with the adjacent sectors.

00:07:29.364 --> 00:07:32.927
It's also common to add a clearance controller or a track controller.

00:07:34.227 --> 00:07:37.083
Now the handoff points, starting on the Gander side.

00:07:37.083 --> 00:07:39.225
You'll hand traffic to five neighbours.

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:43.673
To the north is Reykjavik, callsign Reykjavik Control, run by VATSIM Scandinavia.

00:07:43.673 --> 00:07:47.837
The minimum is ten nautical miles at and above flight level two-seven-zero,

00:07:47.837 --> 00:07:48.670
and five below.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.239
To the south-west is New York Oceanic,

00:07:52.239 --> 00:07:56.002
callsign New York Radio, run by New York ARTCC, part of VATUSA.

00:07:56.002 --> 00:07:57.185
They simulate ADS-C.

00:07:57.185 --> 00:08:03.853
You hand off to its Mercury sector, and New York delegates a block of airspace north of forty-five thirty north,

00:08:03.853 --> 00:08:06.055
between forty and fifty west, to you.

00:08:07.355 --> 00:08:12.967
To the south is Santa Maria, callsign Santa Maria Radio, run by the Portugal vACC.

00:08:12.967 --> 00:08:17.894
You hand off to its non-radar sector, using our normal ADS-C separation.

00:08:19.194 --> 00:08:22.575
On the land side is Montreal, a radar service run by VATCAN.

00:08:22.575 --> 00:08:25.309
Its sectors split at flight level two-nine-zero,

00:08:25.309 --> 00:08:27.758
and the lower ones sit underneath the GOTA.

00:08:27.758 --> 00:08:29.561
The minimum there is five miles.

00:08:29.561 --> 00:08:33.449
Last is Gander Domestic, also VATCAN radar, with a five-mile minimum.

00:08:33.449 --> 00:08:38.351
When Gander Domestic is offline, Moncton Center covers its airspace from top to bottom.

00:08:39.651 --> 00:08:40.885
Now the Shanwick side.

00:08:40.885 --> 00:08:45.036
Reykjavik and Santa Maria also border Shanwick, run by the same divisions.

00:08:45.036 --> 00:08:50.085
You hand off to Santa Maria's non-radar sector here too, with our normal ADS-C separation.

00:08:50.085 --> 00:08:52.516
Reykjavik also controls the RATSU triangle,

00:08:52.516 --> 00:08:57.209
which Scottish delegates to it at the junction of Reykjavik, Scottish and Shanwick.

00:08:58.509 --> 00:09:03.201
On the eastern edge, first is Scottish Control, a radar service run by VATSIM UK.

00:09:03.201 --> 00:09:07.371
It borders Shanwick at ten degrees west, between Reykjavik and the NOTA.

00:09:07.371 --> 00:09:11.629
The minimum is seven nautical miles laterally for aircraft on ATS routes,

00:09:11.629 --> 00:09:15.712
and fifteen miles longitudinally, dropping to five with speed control.

00:09:15.712 --> 00:09:20.462
Shanwick coordinates east and westbound traffic at GOMUP, on the edge of the NOTA.

00:09:20.462 --> 00:09:25.212
If westbound traffic is going to Shannon rather than Shanwick, tell Scottish West.

00:09:26.512 --> 00:09:30.520
Next is Shannon Control, also radar, run by VATéir, the Irish division.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:34.678
It borders Shanwick from GOMUP counter-clockwise around its FIR to TULTA,

00:09:34.678 --> 00:09:36.842
and it controls the NOTA and the SOTA.

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:38.310
The minimum is five miles.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.511
There's one special transfer condition.

00:09:40.511 --> 00:09:43.183
For eastbound aircraft between SUNOT and BEDRA,

00:09:43.183 --> 00:09:48.357
control transfers at the sector boundary, and communications transfer one degree before it.

00:09:49.657 --> 00:09:53.178
Last are Brest and Madrid, both radar with a five-mile minimum.

00:09:53.178 --> 00:09:57.426
Brest is run by the French vACC, and Madrid by VATSPA, the Spanish division.

00:09:57.426 --> 00:09:59.006
Brest Control runs the BOTA,

00:09:59.006 --> 00:10:03.014
and its main sector covers everything below flight level two-five-five.

00:10:03.014 --> 00:10:06.961
Madrid, callsign Madrid Radar, takes every handoff on its West sector,

00:10:06.961 --> 00:10:09.497
which is split at flight level two-four-five.

00:10:10.797 --> 00:10:13.839
Most traffic is deemed coordinated, which means you don't call.

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:22.326
An aircraft entering your OCA is deemed coordinated if it's at its cleared level, will cross the entry point within three minutes of the last estimate or the time restriction,

00:10:22.326 --> 00:10:24.508
and the receiving controller hasn't objected.

00:10:24.508 --> 00:10:27.715
Leaving the OCA, the aircraft must be at its flight-planned level,

00:10:27.715 --> 00:10:32.088
with no level change in the last five minutes before the exit point and no known conflict.

00:10:33.388 --> 00:10:35.178
There are two ways to transfer a tag.

00:10:35.178 --> 00:10:38.781
With the silent method, you transfer communications together with the tag,

00:10:38.781 --> 00:10:42.822
and the receiving controller accepts the tag only once the aircraft has checked in.

00:10:42.822 --> 00:10:46.596
This is used between oceanic sectors, and between Shanwick and its neighbours.

00:10:46.596 --> 00:10:50.007
With the confirmed method, you start the tag transfer and wait for the

00:10:50.007 --> 00:10:53.224
receiving controller to accept before transferring communications.

00:10:53.224 --> 00:10:55.788
This is used between Gander and the domestic sectors.

00:10:57.088 --> 00:10:58.191
That's the airspace.

00:10:58.191 --> 00:11:01.888
In the next video, we'll look at routes, clearances and separation.
